 {"q": "In the covenant ceremony of Genesis 15, why does it matter that only the smoking furnace and flaming torch pass between the pieces?", "options": ["It shows that Abram was not yet worthy to take part, because he had asked for a sign", "It shows that God alone takes on the self-curse, so the covenant depends on God keeping his word, not on Abram", "It shows the ceremony was a sacrifice to atone for Abram's sins", "It shows that Abram would have to keep the law before he could share in the covenant"], "answer": 1, "why": "Passing between the pieces meant 'may I be torn apart if I fail'. Only God does it."},
 {"q": "Paul argues from the order of events in Genesis. Which is his point in Romans 4:10–11?", "options": ["Abraham was circumcised before he believed", "Abraham was counted righteous before he was circumcised, so he can be the father of uncircumcised believers too", "Abraham was counted righteous because he was circumcised", "Gentiles must be circumcised to share Abraham's faith"], "answer": 1, "why": "Genesis 15:6 comes before Genesis 17; Paul uses that to include Gentiles."},
 {"q": "Which is the best account of how Paul and James can both cite Genesis 15:6?", "options": ["One of them misquoted it", "They use 'works' differently: Paul rejects works of the law as a way to earn acceptance, James rejects a 'faith' that never acts", "James is speaking of Isaac's faith, not Abraham's", "Paul wrote to Jews and James to Gentiles, so different rules apply"], "answer": 1, "why": "Most interpreters, including the Joint Declaration of 1999, read them as complementary."},
 {"q": "What did the Joint Declaration of 1999 agree?", "options": ["That the Catholic Church accepts all of Luther's teaching", "That Lutherans and Catholics agree in substance that we are accepted by God by grace alone through faith in Christ, not by merit, while differences of emphasis remain", "That we are justified by works, which complete faith", "That Catholic, Lutheran and Orthodox teaching are now identical"], "answer": 1, "why": "It was a real but limited agreement, later joined by Methodists, Anglicans and Reformed churches."},
